## INTRODUCTION
Two-dimensional metal−organic frameworks (2D MOFs) form structures in which the atoms are connected as a single layer, and these layers are stacked on top of one another via van der Waals (vdW) interactions.1−3 Compared to other 2D materials such as graphene, MXenes, transition-metal dichalcogenide, and metal oxides,4−10 2D MOFs differentiate themselves with hierarchical porosity, high tunability, and high surface area.11 Recently, conductive 2D MOFs with metallic ions and conjugated organic linkers have been attracting promising attention because of their excellent electrical properties caused by strong charge delocalization through extended $\pi{-}\mathrm{d}$ conjugation and high charge carrier mobility and concentration.12−16 Ever since Hmadeh et al. reported electrical charge transport pathways in conductive 2D MOFs,17 many different 2D MOFs have been synthesized using various metals (e.g., Co, Ni, Cu, Pt, Pd, and Fe), ligands (e.g., benzene, triphenylene, trinaphthylene, and tetraazanaphthotetraphene), and functional groups (e.g., $-\mathrm{OH}_{\mathit{i}}$ , $\mathrm{-NH}_{2},$ $-S\mathrm{H}_{\mathrm{\ell}}$ , and $-S\mathrm{eH}{\dot{\bf\theta}}_{,}$ ).14 Consequently, conductive 2D MOFs are considered promising materials for various electrical
applications such as chemical sensors,18−22 electrocatalysis,23−27   
and energy storage.28−32
Among the different characteristics possessed by the 2D MOFs, the stacking mode depends on the arrangement of adjacent vdW-stacked layers and serves as an important parameter in this class of materials. Notable stacking modes in 2D MOFs include (1) eclipsed stacking (i.e., here, AA stacking; completely overlapping neighboring layers) (Figure 1a), (2) slipped parallel (i.e., here, AB stacking; slightly displaced adjacent layers) (Figure 1b), (3) staggered (i.e., neighboring layers being displaced about half of the unit cell) (Figure 1c), and (4) inclined (i.e., the cascaded arrangement of layers) (Figure 1d).33 Furthermore, Jiang et al. recently reported a bifunctional 2D MOF (i.e., copper 1,3,5-triamino-2,4,6-benzenetriol $\left(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{TABTO})_{2}\right)^{\cdot}$ ), which possesses a unique and novel rotated stacking mode, in which neighboring layers are rotated $180^{\circ}$ with respect to each other (Figure 1e).34 As such, 2D MOFs can have various stacking modes depending on the level of displacement and the arrangement of neighboring layers.
It is interesting to note that despite having the same chemistry, a change in the stacking mode for a given MOF can lead to different properties. Recently, Cao et al. reported the diffusion mechanism of water molecules in different types of conductive 2D MOFs.35 The water diffusion coefficient in AB stacking was $21.5\%$ lower than in AA stacking, and the reduced value in AB stacking was caused by the higher number of hydrogen bonds near the inner surface and the zig-zag geometry. The hexagonal one-dimensional (1D) pore in honeycomb topology 2D MOFs is blocked or open according to the stacking mode that directly affects the geometrical and chemical properties. Meanwhile, Kuc et al. calculated the corresponding electronic band structures for the AA and AB stacking mode of Cu-SURMOF-2 (i.e., the Cu paddle-wheel attached with four 1,4-benzene dicarboxylate linkers) 2D MOF.36 Although the in-plane bands were nearly dispersion-less for all stackings, band dispersion in the out-ofplane direction was induced by the stacking mode change from AA to AB, which corresponds to the increased p-orbital contribution of C atoms in linkers. The fully eclipsed and slightly slipped neighboring 2D sheets may provide the various charge transport pathways, with varying $\pi{-}\pi$ orbital overlap and $\pi{-}\mathrm{d}$ conjugation. Therefore, it is important to characterize the stacking mode of 2D MOFs in order to fully understand their intrinsic properties.
Although some mechanism studies behind the different arrangements of the stacking modes in 2D MOFs have been reported,37,38 the stacking mode characterization still solely relies on empirical results and measurements from experiments and cannot be reliably predicted prior to experimental synthesis.
Even though state-of-the-art analytical techniques such as powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD), single-crystal X-ray diffraction, extended X-ray absorption fine structure (EXAFS), highresolution transmission electron microscopy, and K-edge X-ray absorption near edge structure can be used to characterize the stacking modes accurately, they are available only after the materials synthesis.22,39−41 For this reason, the development of a reliable way to predict stacking mode is imperative to “screen” for materials with user-desired stacking modes in the 2D MOFs prior to the synthesis.
Several computational methods have been combined with the experimental results to characterize the stacking mode such as calculating PXRD patterns of crystal models39,40 or measuring metal−metal distance in conjunction with EXAFS results.22,40,41 Very recently, Zhang et al. presented 1072 DFT-optimized structures of conductive 2D MOFs, which included considerations of their stacking mode.42 One viable solution to predict stacking mode in advance is to use a potential energy surface (PES) map, which is constructed by density functional theory (DFT). We can intuitively identify the stacking mode with just a simple energy comparison using the DFT PES map. Sheberla et al. reported the first DFT PES map using periodic single-point energy calculations for 82 displaced $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(2,3,6,7,10,1$ 1-hexaiminotriphenylene) $_2$ (here, Ni-HITP) 2D MOF structures and showed that the minimum energy configuration obtained from the DFT results agreed with the experimentally determined AB stacking mode.40 Also, Huang et al. revealed the DFT PES map for copper bis(dithiolene) (here, Cu-HTB) with $\mathord{\sim}1000$ different displacements,43 and Clough et al. employed the PBEsol exchange−correlation functional to the iron and cobalt 2,3,6,7,10,11-triphenylenehexathiolate (here, Fe-HTTP and Co-HTTP).44,45 Although the DFT PES map is a powerful tool to predict unknown stacking modes of synthesized or nonsynthesized 2D MOFs, a limited number of DFT PES maps are currently available in the literature. Therefore, constructing a universal DFT PES map database for a larger number of 2D MOFs is necessary and can provide a deep understanding of the stacking mode formation mechanism and their corresponding chemical/electrical properties.
In this work, we first report a highly accurate and coherent DFT PES map database that can predict the stacking modes of 36 conductive 2D MOFs. The 2D MOFs that were generated are considered three types of transition metals, i.e., Co, Ni, and Cu; three types of functional groups $-\mathrm{OH}$ $(\mathrm{X}=\mathrm{H})$ , $\ensuremath{-}\ensuremath{\mathrm{N}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}_{2}}$ ( $\mathbf{\nabla}\mathrm{X}=$ I), and $-S\mathrm{H}$ $\mathbf{\check{X}}=\mathbf{T}\mathbf{\Psi})$ ; and four types of conjugated linkers hexaX-benzene (HXB), 2,3,6,7,10,11-hexa-X-triphenylene (HXTP), 2,3,8,9,14,15-hexa-X-trinaphthylene (HXTN), and 2,3,7,8,12,13-hexa-X-tetraazanaphthotetraphene (HXTT) (Figure 1f). For the case study, hydrogen sulfide $\left(\operatorname{H}_{2}S\right)$ was employed as a guest molecule to compute the host−guest interaction with the 2D MOFs. Hydrogen sulfide is a toxic gas with a characteristic smell that causes irritation to the eyes or respiratory system even at a concentration of about 10 parts per million (ppm).46−48 We analyzed the $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ binding energy differences for AA and AB stacking modes of the 2D MOFs and observed that the host−guest interaction was enhanced as AA became AB stacking mode. Furthermore, we explored the electrical charge transport pathway for AA and AB stacking modes of 2D MOFs, wherein the charge transport of AB stacked 2D MOFs was reduced through in-plane direction, but maintained or elevated through the out-of-plane direction, resulting in the high electrical conductivity.
## EXPERIMENTAL SECTION
extended layer (i.e., $\mathrm{M}_{3}(\mathrm{linker})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{6})$ and (2) discrete complex (i.e., M3(linker) (H2O)12).15,17,56 The extended layer is composed of divalent metal cluster centers with a four-coordinated functional group in a square planar geometry, but the discrete complex contains an octahedral geometry of metal ions with only two-coordinated functional groups and four-coordinated $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules. Here, only extended layers were considered because defining the stacking mode of the discrete complex containing 2D MOFs is ambiguous. (See details in Supporting Information Note 2) In addition, by assuming that the 2D MOFs are fully activated, all $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules bonded to the axial position of the metal cluster were removed. (See details in Supporting Information Note 3) At first, the initial structures were optimized by the Forcite module in Materials Studio 2019 software58 to provide reliable input for DFT optimization. Then, DFT geometrical optimization was performed so that each structure had optimized cell parameters and inter-layer distance by relaxing the cell parameter, volume, and ionic position altogether. The optimized cell parameters and AA stacking structures are shown in Table S1 and Figure S1, respectively. Meanwhile, most of the recently reported slipped parallel AB stacking 2D MOFs showed displacement within ${\sim}2\bar{\mathrm{~A~}}$ according to the a and $b$ axis lattice vector.40,41,59 Therefore, $2.0\mathring\mathrm{A}$ displacement to both axes was given in one layer in AA stacking and then geometrical optimization with fixed cell parameters and volume was performed to obtain optimized AB stacking structures.
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
Stacking Mode Prediction Using the DFT PES Map Database. The DFT PES map database of 36 2D MOFs is shown in Figure 2. The calculation details are presented in Supporting Information Note 1. For validation purposes, our results were compared with the previously reported DFT PES map of Ni-HITP,40 Co-HTTP,44 and $\dot{\mathrm{Cu-HTB}}^{43}$ structures (Figure S2). Although absolute energy values slightly differ, the locations of the minimum and maximum potential energy regions and the overall energy distribution patterns showed good agreement. A comparative analysis with the recently reported EC-MOF database was also performed.42 (Supporting Information Note 4, Table S5) Next, the DFT-predicted and the experimentally reported stacking modes were compared for 19 experimentally reported 2D MOFs. Among these MOFs, CoHHTP, Ni-HHTP,17 Co-HHTT, and Ni-HHTT56 are known to have discrete complexes and, as such, were excluded from the analysis due to the ambiguity in determining their stacking modes (see details in the Experimental Section). Also, Ni-HTB was ruled out because it has been reported that the adjacent layers are staggered and rotated by $60^{\circ}$ , which is not suitable for our system.60 From the remaining set of 14 MOFs, AA stacking was assumed when the global minimum energy configuration was identified within the small displacement of $\bar{0.6\mathring\mathrm{A}}$ along both the $a$ and $b$ axes (where displacement of $0\mathring\mathrm{A}$ indicates the ideal AA structure). Also, a few of the MOFs (i.e., Co-HIB, Cu-HIB, Cu-HTB, Cu-HITP, Cu-HTTP, and Cu-HHTN) showed dual predicted stacking modes where the energy difference between AA and AB was less than $20\%$ . From this rule, the predicted stacking modes as well as experimental stacking modes were tabulated in Table S2. Overall, $92.9\%$ agreement (i.e., 13/14 MOFs) between the theoretical and the experimental stacking modes was achieved, with the lone exception being Cu-HHTT (i.e., for $\mathtt{C u}$ -HHTT, AB stacking is predicted, but AA stacking is reported). The experimental inter-layer distance of $\mathtt{C u}$ -HHTT in AA stacking (i.e., metal−metal distance) is reported to be 3.19 $\pm0.02\mathring{\mathrm{A}},$ which is smaller than other 2D MOFs probably due to the strong $\pi{-}\pi$ interaction caused by the tetraaza group.56 Meanwhile, the inter-layer distance of Cu-HHTT in AA stacking was obtained to be $3.37\mathrm{~\AA~}$ in our study (Table S1). The difference in inter-layer distance values may account for the discrepancy because a similar phenomenon was reported: CuHHTP, widely known as AB stacking, may also become AA stacking when the inter-layer distance is close to $3.16\mathring{\mathrm{A}}.^{61}$ The significance of inter-layer spacing in influencing the stacking mode is clearly demonstrated by the Ni-HIB example (Figure S3). Although our prediction is not completely consistent with the experimental results, the DFT PES map clearly showed high predictability for the stacking modes. In addition, we constructed a PES map database using the classical universal force field (UFF) method to evaluate whether a less computationally demanding methodology such as UFF could provide a sufficiently accurate preliminary analysis for the stacking modes of 2D MOFs as well as to verify the consistency between DFT PES and UFF PES (Figure S4). Our analysis showed that there was a negligible agreement between the factors under comparison, and ab-initio calculations are necessary when predicting the stacking mode of the 2D MOFs.
Host−Guest Interaction in AA and AB. The DFT calculation results for single $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ binding energy and charge transfer (CT) are shown in Figure 3. (The mechanism study for the two $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ is discussed in Supporting Information Note 5) The 2D MOFs that share the same ligand and the functional groups possess similar binding energy values regardless of the metal type (Figure 3a). For example, 2D MOFs of $C o$ -HIB, Ni-HIB, and Cu-HIB have binding energies of −27.5, −28.6, and $-23.9$ $\mathrm{kJ/mol}$ for AA stacking, respectively. The result may indicate that an effect of the ligand is more dominant in the linker site compared to that of the metal in determining the host−guest interaction with the $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ molecule. Notably, it is interesting to note that the binding energies were greatly enhanced by the transition from AA to AB stacking. The calculated binding energies of $C_{0}$ -HIB, Ni-HIB, and ${\mathrm{Cu-HIB}}$ in the AB were $-49.8,$ −49.3, and $-47.9\mathrm{kJ/mol},$ respectively, which were increased of 81, 72, and $101\%$ compared to the AA. Since $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ has a kinetic diameter of $3.6\textup{\AA},^{62}$ it is difficult to freely penetrate along the adjacent layers of the AA stacking 2D MOFs (i.e., inter-layer distances are $3.0{-}3.5\mathrm{~\AA},$ , Table S1). In AB stacking mode, however, the displacement of the A and B layers can potentially carve out room for $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ to roam around the small pockets that optimize the nearest neighbor distance interactions between the $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ molecule and the MOF atoms (Figure S5). This can explain the stronger $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ binding energy in AB as compared to AA. It is also worth mentioning that the 2D MOFs composed of the $\ensuremath{-}\ensuremath{\mathrm{NH}_{2}}$ functional group showed a larger increase of binding energy by the transition from AA stacking to AB stacking as compared to the −OH and $-S\mathrm{H}$ functionalized 2D MOFs. This result is mainly attributed to the increased strength of hydrogen bonding between the positive H atom of the $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ and the negative N atom of the $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ functional group as the stacking mode changed to AB (Figure S6).63 Therefore, the stacking mode must be carefully considered when using 2D MOFs as gas adsorption layers since the amount of host−guest interaction can be controlled by varying the stacking mode.
Furthermore, Bader charge analysis was performed to identify the CT phenomenon. The amount of CT between $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ and 2D MOFs is presented in Figure 3b. In general, the amount of CT was larger in AB compared to that of AA, except for M-HHB. In the AA/AB stacking mode, the CT occurred from MOF to $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ with the amount of $0.016e/0.032e,\ 0.02e/0.037e.$ , and $0.001e/$
$0.029e$ for Co-HIB, Ni-HIB, and $\mathrm{Cu-HIB}_{\mathrm{.}}$ , respectively. The increase in CT amount indicates that the electronic interaction between the host and guest was promoted in the AB stacking, which is consistent with the enhancement of binding energy. Interestingly, CT directions as well as the amount of CT varied depending on the type of 2D MOFs. $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ showed electrondonating characteristics in the $-\mathrm{OH}$ functionalized 2D MOFs. Yet, the $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ acted as an electron acceptor with the $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ or $-S\mathrm{H}$ functionalized 2D MOFs with negative CT values. Therefore, the donor−acceptor chemistry varies according to the ligand type of 2D MOFs or the type of gas molecule.
Electronic Structure in AA and AB. Thus far, we have investigated the stacking mode prediction using the DFT PES map database and the host−guest interaction change with external $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ according to the stacking mode. As the AB stacking tendency increased, stronger binding energy and an increased amount of CT were obtained. However, conductive 2D MOFs possess intrinsic conductivity and carrier mobility promoted by $\pi{-}\mathrm{d}$ and $\pi{-}\pi$ conjugated orbital overlap. For that reason, changes in stacking mode affect not only the interaction with external components but also the intrinsic electronic structure inside the 2D MOFs.
The intrinsic conductivity and electronic structure in conductive 2D MOFs can be deduced from the computational DFT band structure calculations. The band structures of M-HIB and M-HITP with respect to the stacking modes are illustrated in Figure $\scriptstyle4\ a-c$ and Figures S7 and S8. Except for Ni-HIB AB $(2.0,\ 2.0\ \mathring\mathrm{A})$ , all band structures showed metallic behavior (i.e., zero bandgap). However, Ni-HIB AB $\left(2.0,2.0\mathring\mathrm{A}\right)$ also possessed very small indirect and direct bandgap values of 0.018 and 0.089 eV, respectively, showing semi-metallic behavior (Figure 4b). The extremely small or zero bandgap for M-HIB and M-HITP implied that these 2D MOFs may have high electrical conductivity. Strikingly, our DFT band structures revealed that the frontier bands crossing or aligning near the Fermi level exhibit a notable decrease in band dispersion as stacking mode changes from AA to AB (red-shaded line in Figure $\mathrm{4a-c}$ ). For the Ni-HIB, the Fermi crossing frontier band for AA and AB $(4.2,2.0\mathring\mathrm{A})$ and the conduction band minimum for AB (2.0, 2.0 Å) were used to calculate the band dispersion, and the results are tabulated in Table S3. The strong band dispersion highly correlates with the strong orbital overlap for the neighboring atoms, which is ideal for charge transport (i.e., the amount of active delocalized free electrons decreased as band dispersion decreased). In the AA stacking of Ni-HIB, $314~\mathrm{meV}$ of average band dispersion along all $k$ -points is calculated, but the 155 and $124\mathrm{meV}$ of band dispersion are calculated at the AB $(2.0,2.0\mathring\mathrm{A})$ and AB (4.2, 2.0 Å), respectively. In other words, the orbital conjugation ability of the metal cluster and organic ligand was maximized at the AA stacking, suggesting that it is the optimal configuration with strongly enhanced delocalization. For the case of AB stacking mode, the systematical orbital delocalization was disconnected and the charge transport pathway was reduced.
For the highly conjugated 2D MOFs, there are two types of charge transport pathways: (1) in-plane (i.e., through bonds) and (2) out-of-plane (i.e., through space).13,64 In general, it is thought that the stacking mode affects the out-of-plane charge transport pathway without affecting the in-plane charge transport pathway because only the arrangement of adjacent layers is changed and the layer itself is maintained. Unexpectedly, it is noteworthy that the stacking mode change showed a significant effect on the in-plane charge transport rather than the out-of-plane charge transport. The average band dispersion of Ni-HIB along the in-plane $k$ -points was calculated as 323, 159, and $107~\mathrm{{meV}}$ in the AA, AB (2.0, $2.0\mathring\mathrm{A},$ , and AB (4.2, 2.0 Å), respectively (Table S3). The in-plane band dispersion was reduced as AB characteristics were stronger. Hence, overlap disturbance of adjacent layers caused by stacking displacement highly affects in-plane $\pi{-}\mathrm{d}$ orbital conjugation. Intriguingly, compared with in-plane charge transport, a different trend was observed for out-of-plane charge transport. The average band dispersion of Ni-HIB along the out-of-plane $k$ - points to the AA, AB (2.0, 2.0 Å), and AB $(\bar{4}.2,2.0\mathring{\mathrm{A}})$ was 260, 129, and $228{\mathrm{~meV}},$ , respectively (Table S3). The out-of-plane band dispersion in AB $\bar{(4.2,2.0\mathrm{{\AA})}}$ was greater than in AB (2.0, $2.0\mathring\mathrm{A}\big)$ . It is emphasized that the in-plane and out-of-plane charge transport pathways behave differently depending on the stacking displacement. For the Ni-HITP, the in-plane average band dispersion was deduced as 190, 108, and $110\mathrm{meV}$ to the AA, AB (2.0, 2.0 Å), and AB (3.4, 3.4 Å), respectively. Yet, out-of-plane average band dispersion was calculated as 132, 41, and $262\mathrm{meV}$ (Table S4). Similar to the Ni-HIB, in-plane band dispersion was decreased, but out-of-plane dispersion was maintained or rather promoted as the stacking mode changed from AA to AB. The charge transport pathway differences in the distinct stacking structures of Ni-HIB were visually verified through decomposed charge density plots (Supporting Information Note 11, Figure S9). Meanwhile, in the real world, it is improbable that a completely flat surface can be maintained, as some distortion is expected.17,51,65 We performed band structure calculations on the distorted $\mathtt{C u}$ -HIB. Intriguingly, the distorted $\mathtt{C u}$ -HIB also exhibited a reduction in in-plane band dispersion with an increasing AB tendency, while the out-of-plane band dispersion remained nearly consistent across the three stacking modes (Figure S10).
## CONCLUSIONS
In this research, we have conducted an in-depth analysis of the stacking mode of highly conjugated conductive 2D MOFs using DFT. The DFT PES map database was constructed, and our results indicate an accuracy of $92.9\%$ in the stacking mode prediction. Moreover, we found that the 2D MOFs that have been experimentally reported as both AA and AB stacking were thermodynamically reversible due to the small DFT energy difference between each of the stacking modes. Next, taking $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ as a case study, it was revealed that the host−guest interaction becomes stronger upon transition from the AA to AB stacking mode for a given MOF. Furthermore, the electronic band structure calculation confirmed that as AB stacking displacement became larger, in-plane charge transport was reduced, but the out-of-plane charge transport was maintained or even increased. Our theoretical study reveals that high conductivity can be secured even in 2D MOFs with AB stacking. Clearly, there is a complex trade-off between interaction and conductivity according to the stacking mode. However, it is possible that 2D MOFs with AB stacking can be more advantageous rather than AA stacking in real-world applications such as gas adsorption. As a case study, the $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ gas molecule exhibited a strong interaction with 2D MOFs in the AB stacking mode.
Here, we only considered two types of stacking modes, i.e., eclipsed AA stacking and slipped parallel AB stacking. However, various stacking modes such as staggered, inclined, and rotated were recently reported. Furthermore, factors such as inter-layer distance or layer flatness may influence the stacking mode. Thus, to account for a wider range of potential variables, it is necessary to expand the DFT PES map database in future work. Beyond this, the significance of this study is that it provides practical insights to many experimental researchers when characterizing stacking mode and propels the community to form the stacking mode as an important tunable variable for various 2D MOF applications.
